  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Represent the solution of the inequality $2>3x+5$ on a number line.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & >3x+5 \\\\ 2-5 & >3x+5-5 \\\\ -3 & >3x+0 \\\\ -3 & >3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & >3x \\\\ \\frac{-3}{3} & >\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{-3}{3} & >\\frac{\\cancel{3}x}{\\cancel{3}} \\\\ -1 & >x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side. Remember, we also need to flip the inequality.\n\n$$\nx<-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x<-1.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14248115/assets/093f2e5ade9371ae84fa09ab6bee24b18930a781aac7a5fd16c3dd35fecae5ab.png)",
